0 . In old burnt sites, on burnt peat or soil; sp rugulose-verruculose (fig. 793B). Cap 5-20(-29)  mm, bell-shaped to convex-umbonate, when fully expanded depressed around an umbo,  when young with incurved margin and with veil remnants at margin, fatty-shiny, faintly  striate up to 1/3 of radius, rusty brown to dark ochre brown;/// Gills adnate, coloured ± like  the cap, paler when young; stem 15-35(-80) x 1-3 mm, pale brown, downwards darker, grey  brown with age; veil remnants as minute fibrils, occasionally as a weak ring-zone; smell and  taste indistinct/// . Sp 9-10(-11.5) x 5-5.5(-6) µm, narrowly amygdaloid-ellipsoid, with perispore  leaving an apically outdrawn, smooth part and ± distinct 'ears', with distinct plage, yellow  brown; cheilocystidia (22-)30-40(-50) x (8-)10-13(-15) x 3-5(-8) µm, lageniform to lageniform- fusiform. Subfasciculate and gregarious among low mosses and other carbonicolous fungi;  autumn; rare in hemib.; SE (LC, Vg: Trollhättan, Srm: Österhaninge). - Jordst 17(2), Phil 228d,  St 17 025495137630 G. carbonicola A.H. Sm. (G. phillipsii D.A. Reid)0
0 Not in burnt sites; sp practically smooth to minutely verruculose-punctate 	402549523765026
0 Stem (20-)50-100 x 1-3(-4) mm; cap 6-20(-30) mm02652533866027
0 Stem 5-50 x 0.5-1(-2) mm; cap 0.5-15 mm02652543867028
0 Lignicolous on woody debris or needles; veil rather copious; sp somewhat angular with a  long, conical apex in front view (fig. 793C). Cap 10-12(-30) mm, expanded with a distinct  umbo, dark red brown, striate at margin;/// Gills adnate or with small tooth, yellow brown;  stem 20-40(-70) x 1-3(-4) mm, ± widened at base, beige to brown, darkening greyish from  base, with weak ring-zone and ± patches and fibrils from veil; smell and taste farinaceous.  Sp 10.5-12 x 6.5-7.5 µm, amygdaloid-angular, with a distinct plage, with 'ears' at base and in  apical part, tawny to dark rusty brown; cheilocystidia 33-65 x 5-10 x 3-5 x 4-7 µm, subcylindrical  to lageniform with blunt to subcapitate apices. Recorded on Picea; autumn; very rare  in hemib.; NO (Akh: Oslo). 027535539680 G. subarctica A.H. Sm. & Singer0
0 Sphagnicolous; veil evanescent, only leaving minute fibrils on stem; sp not angular (fig.  793D). Cap bluntly conical to bell-shaped, expanding to bluntly umbonate, striate almost  to centre, shiny, young red brown, becoming yellow brown to ochre brown with a darker  and more fulvous umbo;/// Gills emarginate-adnate to slightly decurrent with tooth, yellowish  brown; stem 35-100 x 1-3(-4) mm, cylindrical, somewhat undulating, pale yellowish brown  with a paler base; smell and taste indistinct/// . Sp 9-11(-12) x 6-7.5 µm, ellipsoid to broadly  ovoid, with a small pore or callus, smooth, with basal 'ears' or with perispore ± loosening  around entire basal part, with distinct plage, tawny; cheilocystidia 30-60 x 6-11 µm, variable,  mostly lageniform with flexuous necks and acute to subcapitate, rarely capitate apices. In  bogs; autumn; rare in hemib.; FI (EH: Siikaneva), NO (LC), SE (LC, Sm: Femsjö). - M&J 1. 027535639690 G. sphagnicola (G.F. Atk.) A.H. Sm. & Singer570
0 the likewise sphagnicolous G. farinacea A.H. Sm. has no veil, a farinaceous smell and taste, and  smaller spores, 8-10(-12) x 5-6 µm, and may be distinct, but many species has been described in this  complex and observations on smell and taste are generally missing or arbitrary.027535739700N0
0 Sp up to 10-10.5 µm long, weakly to distinctly calyptrate with mostly small blisters, often  only near apex and in few sp (fig. 795A). Cap 5-15 mm, broadly conical-paraboloid, expanding  to bell-shaped and convex-umbonate, rusty ochraceous brown, fading to yellow  brown, striate almost to centre, fatty-shiny;/// Gills adnate to slightly decurrent with a tooth,  ochraceous brown to rusty brown; stem 15-35 x 1-1.5(-2) mm, with bulbillose base, with  evanescent veil fibrils, pale yellowish at apex, downwards rusty brown; smell and taste  farinaceous/// . Sp (7.5-)8-10(-10.5) x (4.5-)5-5.5(-6) µm, finely verruculose to marbled, tawny;  cheilocystidia 30-45 x 6-12 x 5-8 µm, ± lageniform. On various mosses, on the ground in forests  and often on moss-covered stumps and logs; common in temp.-suba.; DK (LC), FI (LC),  NO (LC), SE (LC). - FND 23:4, M&J 11, Ves 378. 028545840710 G. hypnorum (Schrank : Fr.) Kühner s. Horak 2005 and de Haan & Walleyn 2006590
0  the likewise small-spored G. fallax A.H. Sm. & Singer, occasionally with blisters, and G. fennica  A.H. Sm (described on a single, dry collection, particular by a cap-like thickening over the spore apex)  have similar cystidia and are recorded from NO and FI respectively by Smith & Singer (1964), both  growing on Sphagnum.028545940720N0
0 Sp > 10.5 µm long, distinctly calyptrate02854604073029
0 Cap at first narrow and acutely to bluntly conical, expanded with a prominent papilla; stem  up to 20 mm long, dark red brown from base; veil relatively well developed. Cap 3-9 mm,  striate up to 2/3 of radius, fatty-shiny, ochre brown to yellow brown, with ± veil remnants  at margin;/// Gills rounded to adnate, initially pale yellow, then ochre to yellow brown; stem  5-20 x 0.5-1 mm, cylindrical, often bent, pale yellow brown at top, with veil remnants;  smell and taste indistinct/// . Sp 10.5-13 x 5.5-7 µm, often with blisters at both ends, practically  smooth, tawny; cheilocystidia 35-50 x 5-10 x 3-8 µm, lageniform with blunt to somewhat  inflated apex. On and among small mosses, on peat, soil and rotting, moss-covered coniferous  wood; autumn; occasional in temp.-bore.; NO (LC), SE (LC). - B&K 5:423, M&J 3. 029606141750 G. calyptrospora Kühner (G. sahleri (Quél.) Kühner s. Favre 1948)0
0 Cap at first bluntly conical-paraboloid, expanding to broadly bell-shaped; stem up to 45  mm long, pale yellow brown to ochraceous brown; veil evanescent02960624176030
0 Young cap fulvous to orange brown; taste farinaceous. Cap 3-15 mm, striate up to 2/3 of the  radius, fatty-shiny, becoming ochre yellow to yellow brown at centre;/// Gills adnate, ochre yellow  to rusty brown; stem 15-45 x 0.5-1.5 mm, often with bulbillose base, pale and becoming  ± yellow ochre or concolorous with the cap, with faint veil remnants/// . Sp 10-12.5 x 6-6.5 µm,  distinctly calyptrate, practically smooth (fig. 795B), orange brown to tawny; cheilocystidia  25-45(-70) x 7-10 x 3-8(-10) µm, lageniform with a blunt to inflated apex. On various mosses,  e.g. Dicranum and Sphagnum, gregarious in acid forests, bogs and ericaceous heathland;  autumn; common in FI, elsewhere occasional in temp.-arc./alp.; DK (LC), FI (LC), FO, NO (LC,  incl. Svb), SE (LC). - B&K 5:411, FND 23:8, IM 79. 030626342770 G. calyptrata P. D. Orton (G. cerina A.H. Sm. & Singer var. calyptrata (P.D. Orton) Arnolds)0
0 Young cap yellow brown to tawny; taste not farinaceous. Cap 5-15 mm, fatty-shiny, striate  up to 2/3 of the radius, red brown to yellow brown at centre, more ochraceous yellow towards  margin;/// Gills adnate, pale ochre to rusty brown; stem 20-45 x 1-2(-3) mm, cylindrical,  pale or ± concolorous with cap, with faint veil remnants/// . Sp 8.5-12.5 x 5.5-6.5(-7.5) µm, with  'ears' around the apiculus, practically smooth, tawny; cystidia 30-40(-50) x 6-12 x 3-5 x 5-10  µm, variable, in general with ± inflated ventral part and broad necks with a blunt to clavate- subcapitate apex. Gregarious on mosses like Polytrichum and Dicranum, also on Sphagnum  and peat, in bogs, acid forests and ericaceous heathland; late spring to autumn; probably  common in temp.-suba.; FI, FO, NO, SE. - FND 23:6. 030626442780 G. cerina A.H. Sm. & Singer650
0 G. cerina has been described with many varieties reflecting considerable variation in shape  and size of cystidia and the veil colour, white or yellow. The sphagnicolous G. leptocystis V.L. Wells &  Kempton, particular by filamentous to narrowly lageniform cheilocystidia with blunt apices, 34-53 x  3.5-6.5 µm, may be just a variety of G. cerina or a small variety of G. sphagnicola - NO.030626542790N0
